# Gemini Code Web - Cursor Rules

## Project Type

This is a Next.js App Router project using:

- Next.js
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Gemini API
- Server-side API routes in `app/api`
- Utility files in `lib`
- Generated project folders in `workspaces`

## Important Files

- `app/page.tsx`: main UI page
- `app/layout.tsx`: root layout
- `app/globals.css`: global CSS
- `app/api/upload/route.ts`: upload ZIP/project API
- `app/api/agent/route.ts`: AI agent API
- `app/api/apply/route.ts`: apply AI changes API
- `app/api/diff/route.ts`: preview diff API
- `app/api/download/route.ts`: download ZIP API
- `lib/gemini.ts`: Gemini client setup
- `lib/workspace.ts`: workspace read/write utilities
- `lib/scanner.ts`: file scanning logic
- `lib/agent.ts`: AI agent logic
- `workspaces/`: uploaded/generated project files

## Coding Rules

- Use TypeScript.
- Use App Router conventions.
- API routes must export `GET`, `POST`, or other HTTP methods from `route.ts`.
- Keep Gemini API key server-side only.
- Never expose `GEMINI_API_KEY` in React client components.
- Use `.env.local` for secrets.
- Prefer simple, readable code.
- Keep UI in `app/page.tsx` unless components are clearly needed.
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling.
- Keep compatibility with Windows paths.
- Do not delete user files in `workspaces` unless explicitly requested.
- When returning API responses, use `NextResponse.json(...)`.
- When handling file paths, validate paths to avoid escaping the workspace directory.

## Current Goal

Build a web-based Gemini code assistant that can:

1. Upload a ZIP/project.
2. Scan files.
3. Ask Gemini to modify code.
4. Preview diff before applying.
5. Apply changes.
6. Generate tests/testcases when needed.
7. Download the modified project as ZIP.

